(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["hemodynamics"]="<h1>Blood Flow and Haemodynamics</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-propels-blood-around-the-circulation\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> What propels blood around the circulation?</h2>\n<p><strong>The heart creates pressure, and blood flows from high pressure to low.</strong> Each beat pushes blood into the arteries at high pressure. The pressure falls step by step through <strong>arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins</strong>, back to the heart at nearly zero.</p>\n<p>Three helpers keep blood moving:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elastic recoil of large arteries.</strong> In systole the aorta stretches like a balloon. In diastole it springs back and keeps pushing blood forward, so flow continues between beats.</li>\n<li><strong>The skeletal muscle pump.</strong> Contracting muscles squeeze the veins. <strong>Valves</strong> in the veins let blood go only towards the heart.</li>\n<li><strong>The respiratory pump.</strong> Breathing in lowers pressure in the chest and draws blood into the large veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These help, but the <strong>heart is the main pressure source</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-actually-determines-flow\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> What actually determines flow?</h2>\n<p><strong>Flow depends on the pressure <em>difference</em>, not the pressure.</strong> Picture two water tanks joined by a pipe. Water flows only if one tank is higher than the other, and faster the bigger the difference. Raise both tanks equally and nothing changes.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Pressure, flow and resistance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Q = (ΔP) divided by (R) R = (ΔP) divided by (Q)\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Q</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Δ</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">P</span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">R</span></span></span><span class=\"eq-gap\" style=\"width:1em\"></span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">R</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Δ</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">P</span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Q</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"equation-example\">Q = blood volume per time; ΔP = upstream − downstream pressure; R = vascular resistance.</span></div>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q</strong> = flow (volume per minute)</li>\n<li><strong>ΔP</strong> = pressure at the start minus pressure at the end</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong> = resistance: how hard the vessels make it for blood to pass</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> a pressure drop of 80 mmHg across a resistance of 20 mmHg·min/L gives 80 ÷ 20 = <strong>4 L/min</strong>.</p>\n<p>So <strong>high pressure alone doesn't mean high flow.</strong> A blocked artery can have high pressure upstream and almost no flow through it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-do-different-vessels-have-different-roles\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Why do different vessels have different roles?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Vessel</th>\n  <th>What its wall is like</th>\n  <th>Its job</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Large arteries</strong></td>\n  <td>Thick and elastic</td>\n  <td>Stretch in systole, recoil in diastole (keep flow going)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Arterioles</strong></td>\n  <td>Thick smooth-muscle layer</td>\n  <td>Control resistance, and so flow into each organ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Capillaries</strong></td>\n  <td>One cell thick</td>\n  <td>Exchange gases, nutrients and fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Veins</strong></td>\n  <td>Thin, stretchy, with valves</td>\n  <td>Hold most of the blood; return it to the heart</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Arterioles are &quot;resistance vessels&quot;</strong> because their muscle can narrow or widen them quickly, and a small change in radius changes resistance hugely (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=6-why-is-vessel-radius-such-a-powerful-determinant-of-flow\">§6</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Veins are &quot;capacitance vessels&quot;</strong> (<em>capacity</em> = how much they hold) because they stretch easily. They can take in a lot of extra blood with little rise in pressure. When sympathetic nerves tighten them, that stored blood is pushed back to the heart (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=10-why-can-veins-hold-more-blood-than-arteries\">§10</a>).</p>\n<p>Capillary structure and lymph are covered in their own chapters. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation\">→ Microcirculation</a>)</em> <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/lymphatics\">→ Lymphatics</a>)</em></p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> One slide gives capillary gaps and fenestrations in millimetres. They are in fact nanometres to micrometres wide.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-why-is-blood-velocity-lowest-in-capillaries\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries?</h2>\n<p><strong>Flow and velocity are different things.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flow (Q)</strong> = <strong>how much</strong> blood passes per minute (L/min).</li>\n<li><strong>Velocity (v)</strong> = <strong>how fast</strong> it moves (cm/s).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Continuity relation</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Q = vA v = (Q) divided by (A)\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Q</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">v</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">A</span><span class=\"eq-gap\" style=\"width:1em\"></span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">v</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Q</span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">A</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"equation-example\">A = total cross-sectional area of the vessels considered.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>A = the total cross-sectional area of all the vessels at that level.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Think of a motorway.</strong> The same number of cars per minute has to pass every point. Where the road is one lane, the cars must go fast. Where it splits into hundreds of lanes, each car can go slowly.</p>\n<p>Each capillary is tiny, but there are billions of them side by side. Their <strong>total</strong> area is far bigger than the aorta's, so blood moves through them <strong>most slowly</strong>. This gives time for exchange. Blood speeds up again as the capillaries join into fewer, larger veins.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 1–4\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–4 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does blood keep flowing in the arteries during ventricular diastole?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricles keep ejecting a little blood in diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The stretched aorta recoils and keeps pushing blood forward</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Valves in the arteries pump blood towards the tissues</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The veins pull blood out of the arteries by suction</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:ebb56462\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The aorta stretches in systole and springs back in diastole, keeping the pressure up and the blood moving.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Pressure falls by 90 mmHg across a vascular bed with a resistance of 30 mmHg·min/L. What is the flow?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 0.3 L/min, from 30 ÷ 90</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 3 L/min, from 90 ÷ 30</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 60 L/min, from 90 − 30</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 2,700 L/min, from 90 × 30</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:d5c0c096\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Q = ΔP ÷ R = 90 ÷ 30 = 3 L/min.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Each capillary is very narrow, yet blood moves slowest in capillaries. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Capillary walls create a lot of friction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Capillaries have the highest blood pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Their total cross-sectional area is enormous</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Red cells block the capillaries completely</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:c96b2f50\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> v = Q ÷ A. The same flow spread across a huge total area moves slowly, like traffic spread across many lanes.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why are veins called capacitance vessels?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They can hold a lot of extra blood with little rise in pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Their muscle controls resistance to blood flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They are the main site of oxygen exchange</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They have the highest pressure in the circulation</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:dde953b4\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Veins stretch easily, so they store large volumes at low pressure. Arterioles are the resistance vessels.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number</h2>\n<p><strong>Laminar flow</strong> means blood moves in smooth layers (<em>lamina</em> = thin layer). The layer touching the wall is held back by friction and moves slowest. Each layer inward slides faster, and the centre is fastest.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure hemo-flow-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 1. Idealised laminar blood-flow profile</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 540 165\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An idealised vessel with multiple flow arrows, the longest arrows at the centre and shortest nearest the vessel wall.\"><rect x=\"40\" y=\"34\" width=\"465\" height=\"99\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9f1ff\" stroke=\"#8caccb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g stroke=\"#2162be\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M89 49H179m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 72H365m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 96H446m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 118H365m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"41\" y=\"22\">Near the wall: slow</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"22\">Near centre: fast</text><text x=\"41\" y=\"153\">Original schematic — not a measured flow profile</text></g></svg><p>The ideal no-slip profile explains the lecturer's arrows. Actual blood contains cells and flowing vessels branch, pulse and deform, so the profile is not always exactly parabolic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Laminar flow is silent.</strong> When flow becomes <strong>turbulent</strong>, swirling in eddies, it makes <strong>noise</strong>: murmurs in the heart, bruits in arteries. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-sounds?section=6-what-makes-a-murmur\">→ Heart Sounds, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>What makes turbulence more likely?</strong> The <strong>Reynolds number (Re)</strong> sums it up:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Reynolds number</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Re = (ρvD) divided by (η)\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Re</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">ρ</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">v</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">D</span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">η</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"equation-example\">ρ = density; v = velocity; D = diameter; η = dynamic viscosity.</span></div>\n\n<p>Turbulence is more likely when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>velocity (v) is high</strong>, as through a narrowed valve or artery;</li>\n<li><strong>the vessel is wide (D)</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>viscosity (η) is low</strong>, as in anaemia, where thinner blood flows more easily.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A high Re makes turbulence <strong>more likely</strong>, not certain. Branching and sudden narrowing also matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Don't confuse R with Re.</strong> R is resistance; Re is the Reynolds number, a pure number with no units.</p>\n<p><strong>Two examples:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anaemia:</strong> thinner blood (lower viscosity) and a faster circulation can cause a <strong><span data-term=\"flow-murmur\">flow murmur</span></strong> with normal valves.</li>\n<li><strong>A narrowed artery:</strong> blood speeds up through the narrow part and becomes turbulent beyond it, causing a <strong><span data-term=\"bruit\">bruit</span></strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-is-vessel-radius-such-a-powerful-determinant-of-flow\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Poiseuille equation</strong> describes smooth flow through a straight tube:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Hagen–Poiseuille equation</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Q = (πΔPr to the power of 4) divided by (8ηL)\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Q</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">π</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Δ</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">P</span><span class=\"eq-script\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">r</span><sup><span class=\"eq-token eq-number\">4</span></sup></span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-number\">8</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">η</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">L</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"equation-example\">r = vessel radius; L = vessel length; η = viscosity.</span></div>\n\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Ideal tube resistance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"R = (8ηL) divided by (πr to the power of 4)\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">R</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-number\">8</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">η</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">L</span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">π</span><span class=\"eq-script\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">r</span><sup><span class=\"eq-token eq-number\">4</span></sup></span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>The key fact: resistance depends on radius to the fourth power (R ∝ 1/r⁴).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What does &quot;to the fourth power&quot; mean?</strong> Multiply the change by itself four times. Halve the radius, and resistance rises by <strong>2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16 times</strong>. So with the same pressure difference, flow falls to <strong>1/16</strong>. Anyone who has tried to drink through a very thin straw knows the feeling.</p>\n<div class=\"hemo-radius-impact\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Effect of radius on ideal vessel resistance\"><div><strong>Radius halved</strong><span>Resistance ×16</span><small>At unchanged ΔP: flow falls to 1/16</small></div><div><strong>Radius doubled</strong><span>Resistance ÷16</span><small>At unchanged ΔP: flow increases sixteenfold</small></div></div>\n\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"radius-dial\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Radius dial</figcaption><p>A slider sets the vessel's radius from 50% to 150% of normal, and the vessel drawing narrows or widens. It shows the radius change, r⁴ worked out step by step, resistance (×) and flow (% of normal, at the same pressure difference), with flow bars for normal and new. It starts at 100%, and presets mark 80% (flow falls to about 41%) and 50% (about 6%). One line: \"A 20% narrowing more than halves flow.\"</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> arterioles control flow by changing their radius only slightly. A 20% narrowing more than halves flow. This is how the body directs blood to the organs that need it (<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics?section=8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels\">§8</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Caution:</strong> Poiseuille assumes a rigid, straight tube and a simple fluid. Blood vessels stretch and branch, and blood contains cells, so the numbers are a guide, not exact predictions.</p>\n<p><strong>A fast jet doesn't mean more flow.</strong> Blood speeds up through a narrowing (v = Q ÷ A), yet the narrowing <strong>raises resistance</strong>, so total flow can <strong>fall</strong>. Speed and flow are different things.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 5–6\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 5–6 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 5–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which change makes turbulent flow most likely?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Lower velocity and higher blood viscosity</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Higher velocity and lower blood viscosity</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A narrower vessel with slower flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Thicker blood in a smooth, straight vessel</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:430f7994\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Re = ρvD ÷ η. Faster flow and thinner blood both raise Re.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A vessel's radius is halved, with pressure, length and viscosity unchanged. What happens to flow?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It halves</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It falls to a quarter</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It falls to an eighth</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It falls to a sixteenth</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:36258547\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> R ∝ 1/r⁴. Half the radius gives 2⁴ = 16 times the resistance, so flow falls to 1/16.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why can anaemia cause a heart murmur with normal valves?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Thinner blood and faster flow make turbulence more likely</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Thicker blood increases friction against the valves</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Low oxygen damages the valve leaflets directly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The red cells clump together and strike the valves</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:b41afb49\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Lower viscosity and a higher cardiac output both raise the Reynolds number, so flow across normal valves can become turbulent.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Blood speeds up as it passes through a narrowed artery. Has total flow through the artery increased?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Yes, because faster blood always means more flow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Yes, because the narrowing lowers resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. No: speed rises because the area falls, while flow may drop</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. No: blood actually slows down in a narrowing</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:41c0ebe3\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> v = Q ÷ A, so speed rises where area falls. The narrowing also raises resistance, so total flow can fall.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"7-viscosity-haematocrit-and-blood-cells\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells</h2>\n<p><strong>Viscosity</strong> is how thick or sticky a fluid is: honey is more viscous than water. Thicker blood flows less easily, so resistance rises.</p>\n<p>Blood viscosity depends mainly on the <strong>haematocrit</strong>, the percentage of blood made up of red cells. It also depends on plasma proteins, temperature and how flexible the red cells are.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polycythaemia</strong> (too many red cells): thicker blood → more resistance → more work for the heart.</li>\n<li><strong>Anaemia</strong> (too few red cells): thinner blood, but each mL carries less oxygen, so the heart pumps more blood to compensate. That also means more work, for a different reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Stiff or misshapen red cells</strong> (sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis) flow less easily through small vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> One slide suggests viscosity has little effect in small vessels. In very narrow vessels, red cells line up in the centre and blood behaves as if it were thinner. This is a real effect, but it depends on vessel size and does not hold everywhere.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels?</h2>\n<p>Vessels are arranged in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"in-series\">In series</span></strong> (one after another, like the arteries then arterioles then capillaries): the resistances <strong>add up</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"in-parallel\">In parallel</span></strong> (side by side, like the many capillaries in a bed, or the organs off the aorta): each extra path <strong>lowers</strong> the total resistance, because blood has more routes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>This solves a puzzle.</strong> Each capillary is narrower than an arteriole, so why aren't capillaries the main resistance? Because there are so many of them <strong>in parallel</strong> that their combined resistance is lower. The <strong>arterioles</strong>, with their muscular walls, are where the biggest pressure drop happens and where resistance can be <strong>adjusted</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>In practice:</strong> when an organ's arterioles constrict, its blood flow falls; when they dilate, its flow rises. The heart's output is the same, but it is shared out differently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-how-can-blood-flow-be-measured\"><span class=\"sec-num\">9.</span> How can blood flow be measured?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Method</th>\n  <th>What it detects</th>\n  <th>How it gives flow</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Electromagnetic flowmeter</strong></td>\n  <td>Voltage produced by blood moving through a magnetic field</td>\n  <td>Voltage rises with flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Doppler ultrasound</strong></td>\n  <td>The change in pitch of sound bouncing off moving red cells</td>\n  <td>Gives <strong>velocity</strong>; vessel area is needed to get flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Plethysmography</strong></td>\n  <td>Swelling of a limb when venous outflow is briefly blocked</td>\n  <td>The rate of swelling equals the arterial inflow</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Doppler measures speed, not volume.</strong> To get flow, multiply velocity by the vessel's cross-sectional area (Q = vA).</p>\n<p>The lecture also gives an <strong>arm-to-tongue circulation time of about 15 seconds</strong>: a substance injected into an arm vein is tasted on the tongue about 15 s later.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-why-can-veins-hold-more-blood-than-arteries\"><span class=\"sec-num\">10.</span> Why can veins hold more blood than arteries?</h2>\n<p><strong>Compliance</strong> is how much a vessel's volume increases for each rise in pressure:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Compliance</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"C = (ΔV) divided by (ΔP)\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">C</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Δ</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">V</span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">Δ</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">P</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<p>Veins are far more compliant than arteries: they balloon out with little pressure. So <strong>most of the blood volume sits in the veins</strong> (about two-thirds at rest), ready to be called on.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does tightening the veins help cardiac output?</strong> Sympathetic nerves make the veins less stretchy. The stored blood is squeezed towards the heart, so <span data-term=\"venous-return\">venous return</span>, <span data-term=\"edv\">EDV</span> and, through Frank–Starling, <span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">stroke volume</span> all rise. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-output?section=4-frank-starling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection\">→ Cardiac Output, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 7–10\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 7–10 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 7–10</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> How do polycythaemia and anaemia both increase the heart's work?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both make the blood thicker and harder to pump</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Polycythaemia thickens blood; anaemia makes the heart pump more blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Both lower blood pressure, so the heart beats faster</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Polycythaemia thins blood; anaemia thickens it</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:3c40d04e\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Thicker blood raises resistance. Anaemic blood is thinner, but carries less oxygen, so cardiac output must rise.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Capillaries are narrower than arterioles. Why are arterioles still the main resistance vessels?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Capillaries have no walls, so they offer no resistance</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Arterioles are longer than all the capillaries put together</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Capillaries are in parallel in huge numbers, and arterioles can adjust their radius</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Blood flows faster through capillaries than through arterioles</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:2e1ee6e0\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> So many parallel capillaries share the flow that their total resistance is low. Arterioles have muscle to change their radius.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> What does Doppler ultrasound measure directly?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The volume of blood flowing per minute</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The pressure difference along the vessel</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The speed of the moving blood cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The viscosity of the blood</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:41b2c690\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Doppler measures velocity from the pitch change. Flow needs the vessel's area as well (Q = vA).</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Sympathetic nerves tighten the veins. What happens to stroke volume?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It rises, because more blood returns to fill the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It falls, because resistance to flow increases</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It is unchanged, because veins don't affect the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It falls, because the heart beats faster</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"hemodynamics:3d4938e1\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Less stretchy veins push stored blood to the heart. More filling means more stroke volume (Frank–Starling).</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"11-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">11.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Flow follows the pressure difference:</strong> Q = ΔP ÷ R. High pressure alone doesn't mean high flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Elastic arteries</strong> recoil in diastole and keep blood moving between beats.</li>\n<li><strong>Vessel roles:</strong> elastic arteries (recoil), arterioles (resistance), capillaries (exchange), veins (capacity).</li>\n<li><strong>Velocity ≠ flow:</strong> v = Q ÷ A. The capillaries' huge total area makes blood slowest there.</li>\n<li><strong>Laminar flow</strong> is silent and fastest at the centre. <strong>Turbulent flow</strong> is noisy (murmurs, bruits).</li>\n<li><strong>Reynolds number</strong> Re = ρvD ÷ η. High velocity, wide vessels and thin blood favour turbulence. Re is not resistance.</li>\n<li><strong>R ∝ 1/r⁴:</strong> halve the radius, and resistance rises 16-fold. A 20% narrowing more than halves flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Viscosity</strong> rises with haematocrit. Polycythaemia and anaemia both raise cardiac work, by different routes.</li>\n<li><strong>Arterioles</strong> are the adjustable resistance. Parallel paths lower total resistance; series resistances add.</li>\n<li><strong>Doppler</strong> measures velocity; flow = velocity × area.</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance</strong> C = ΔV ÷ ΔP. Veins hold most of the blood, and tightening them raises venous return and stroke volume.</li>\n</ol>\n";
